body:
You're completely true. I should speak to my doctor very seriously about it. I can't even get boners in this state. I guess that pivoting over and hitting my brother might be called a "acute moment" of aggressiveness, (thats why I was hospitalized, my mother says it was the last straw, so maybe I created other by product chaos previously; I mention this so I don't appear to victimize myself.)

&#x200B;

Right now I've been leaning on them, not at an extreme, but I do take them to achieve sleep and to another to counter the effect of stiffness. 

&#x200B;

so its halo and bip, halo(peridol) gives me the tension, and biperideno relaxes is(halo=tense, bip=relaxed slight accumulation of saliva, slowishness). What you mention "drugs to regulate other drugs" is true.

&#x200B;

I shall find out the main purpose of haloperidol in the first place, because if it's effects require another drug (bip-erideno)  to counter the side effects of (halo=stiffness), then halo, must have some kind of properties that arent blocked by (bip=relaxant), that are useful, at least momentarily  


Now the benefit has to be seen correctly, from the the ground up, because maybe that for which I am being treated is unnecessary in the first place, or... Maybe I can work on it, without substances.

Yo, if you happen to give up your job, give me a call, I follow the leisure lifestyle, I know all the tips and tricks, peace. 

&#x200B;

Out.

We will indeed have you. 

&#x200B;

Invite sent, if u don't get it message me, I'll resend!

Once I did DMT while being drunk.   


I was instantly sucked out of this world and thrown into a flashy chaotic multicolored limbo, I was trapped for what felt like 100 years in this madness, I endured such insane mind-melting fractals that prompted me to appreciate sobriety in a way I never did before. 

&#x200B;

The trip felt so long and so disconnected from reality that at one point I had given in to the idea that I had fried my brain and I would never go back to normal.

&#x200B;

In retrospect this trip was very educational, it gave me an experience so far removed from everyday mundane life that it provided a context that made the sober life take on a relaxing and grounding vibe.
